<h3>Your deductible: Texas Insurance Code Chapter 707</h3>
<p>Chapter 707 of the Texas Insurance Code makes it illegal for a contractor to pay, waive, absorb or rebate a homeowner's insurance deductible, including through discounts, credits or other incentives meant to offset it. You are responsible for paying your deductible. Be wary of any contractor who offers to "take care of it," because the offer itself is a red flag.</p>
<h3>Public adjusters: Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102</h3>
<p>Chapter 4102 governs public adjusters and prohibits contractors from acting as public adjusters or advertising adjusting services. If you want someone to negotiate your claim, that must be a licensed public adjuster you hire separately, or an attorney. A roofing contractor should never sign as your representative on the claim.</p>

<h2>Storm Claims in Temple and Bell County</h2>
<p>Central Texas has an active spring hail and wind season, roughly March through June, and a secondary storm period in the fall. When a large storm crosses Bell County, insurers and contractors get busy at the same time, and adjuster appointments can take longer to schedule. Most policies have deadlines for reporting damage, so read yours or ask your agent early rather than waiting until a leak appears.</p>
<p>Big storms also attract out-of-area contractors who knock on doors in neighborhoods from Temple to Belton and Harker Heights. Take your time. Ask for photos, a written estimate and local contact information, and never sign anything that assigns your claim or insurance benefits to a contractor without understanding it fully. Our <a href="/weather/">weather page</a> can help you confirm the date of the storm that affected your area, which your insurer will ask for.</p>

<h2>When to Get an Inspection Before Filing</h2>
<p>Schedule an inspection after any storm with hail or damaging winds, before the adjuster visits, and whenever you're unsure whether damage is storm-related or simply age. A professional opinion with photos helps you make an informed choice and avoids filing for damage that may not qualify. For a broader walkthrough, read <a href="/blog/roof-insurance-claims-texas-hailstorm/">understanding roof insurance claims after a Texas hailstorm</a>, and see our <a href="/services/hail-damage-roof-repair-temple-tx/">hail damage roofing</a>, <a href="/services/wind-damage-roof-repair-temple-tx/">wind damage repair</a> and <a href="/services/storm-damage-roof-repair-temple-tx/">storm damage roof repair</a> pages for what we look for on the roof.</p>
